.TH PTY_SAVE_EVIDENCE 3 2026-10-03 "libptytest 0.1" "libptytest Manual"
.SH NAME
pty_save_evidence \- keep the evidence of a failed assertion in files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <ptytest.h>
.PP
.BI "int pty_save_evidence(PtySession *" s ", const char *" dir ", const char *" name ,
.BI "                      const char *" text );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR pty_save_evidence ()
writes three files into the directory
.IR dir ,
creating it if it does not exist (one level only; its parent must exist):
.TP
.IB name .report.txt
.I text
\(em what failed and the comparison report \(em with a final newline;
.TP
.IB name .screen.txt
the screen as
.BR pty_screen_dump (3)
prints it: framed, with row numbers, a column ruler and the cursor;
.TP
.IB name .screen.ansi
the screen with its attributes and colours, as
.BR pty_screen_dump_ansi (3)
writes it: \fBcat\fP shows it in a terminal, and
.BR pty_feed (3)
loads it back into a session of the same size for inspection.
.PP
Files of the same names are replaced.
.I name
must not contain \(oq/\(cq.
.PP
A log keeps what was printed; these files keep what was on the screen,
with its colours, which is what a CI job can attach to a failed run.
.SH THE ASSERTION MACROS
When the environment variable
.B PTYTEST_EVIDENCE_DIR
names a directory, every assertion macro of
.BR pty_assert (3)
that is about a session saves its evidence there when it fails, under the
name
.IB file \- function \-L line
of the assertion, for example
.IR test_inbox.c\-test_delete\-L212 ,
and prints where it went.
A comparison macro's report file holds the full comparison report.
.PP
The tests are responsible for the directory: put it under the project's
build directory, and remove it with the build.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0 on success; \-1 with
.I errno
set if an argument is invalid
.RB ( EINVAL ),
the directory cannot be created, or a file cannot be written completely.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
PTYTEST_EVIDENCE_DIR=$PWD/build/evidence ctest \-L pty
ls build/evidence
cat build/evidence/test_inbox.c\-test_delete\-L212.screen.ansi
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pty_assert (3),
.BR pty_screen_dump (3),
.BR libptytest (7)
